/// Cap values below the at quantile and above the 1 − at quantile.
///
/// Each value brought within the sample quantiles at `at:` and `1 - at`: a value below the
/// lower quantile becomes that quantile, one above the upper becomes that, and the rest stay as
/// they are. It is `clamp(x, quantile(x, at), quantile(x, 1 - at))` with the quantile of type 7,
/// R's default, so that an outlier is pulled in rather than dropped and the count of rows is
/// unchanged. The quantiles are taken over the values present, with the reducers' warning for
/// the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), once for each quantile,
/// and those rows stay `NA`. `at: 0` changes nothing, since the cut points are then the smallest
/// and largest values; `at: 0.5` makes every value the median; a share outside 0 to 0.5 is
/// refused (Z0401). Under `by:` the cut points are taken within each group. Like
/// DescTools' `Winsorize(x, probs = c(at, 1 - at))` and Stata's `winsor2 x, cuts(1 99)`.
///
/// x: The column to winsorise.
/// at: The share cut at each end, between 0 and 0.5; 0.01 unless given, so that the lowest and the highest one per cent are capped.
/// returns: A `Column<Float>` of the same length, `NA` where `x` is.
/// details: Closed form: the two quantiles come from `quantile` under definition 7 and each value is then `min(max(x, low), high)`, exactly R's `pmin(pmax(x, quantile(x, at)), quantile(x, 1 - at))`; nothing else is computed.
/// reference: Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996) Sample quantiles in statistical packages, The American Statistician 50(4), 361–365.
/// reference: Signorell, A., the `Winsorize` function of the DescTools package; Stata's `winsor2` command.
/// missing values: The two cut points are taken over the values present, with a warning for the rows skipped, and a row that is `NA` stays `NA`.
/// common mistake: Writing the share as a percentage. `at:` is the share of the values at each end, so one per cent is 0.01, and five per cent is `winsorize(wage, at: 0.05)`.
/// mistake call: dataset(wages).add(w = winsorize(wage, at: 5))
/// mistake prints: error[Z0401]: `at:` of `winsorize` is a share between 0 and 0.5; above 0.5 the cut points would cross
/// coming from: d.add(w = winsorize(wage, at: 0.01)) | pmin(pmax(d$wage, quantile(d$wage, 0.01, na.rm = TRUE)), quantile(d$wage, 0.99, na.rm = TRUE)) | winsor2 wage, cuts(1 99)
/// coming from: Stata's `winsor2`, a command from outside Stata's own, takes the two cut points as percentiles, 1 and 99, and makes a new variable `wage_w`; here `at:` is a share and the result is named in `add`.
/// related: To pull values into bounds that you give, use `clamp`.
/// related: For the quantiles themselves, use `quantile`.
/// related: To leave the extreme rows out instead, keep the others with `where`.
/// see also: clamp, quantile, bin
/// example: The wages of one year with the lowest and the highest five per cent pulled in: the smallest and largest values before and after.
/// > let w = dataset(wages).where(year == 2019).select([worker, year, wage]).add(capped = winsorize(wage, at: 0.05))
/// > w.aggregate(lowest = min(wage), highest = max(wage), low_capped = min(capped), high_capped = max(capped))
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 1)
/// frame · 1 row × 4 variables
///
///       lowest   highest   low_capped   high_capped
///   1     5.42    104.59       13.084       59.3785
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 2)
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in capped were skipped (line 2)
/// note: the panel declaration `worker × year` dropped: `aggregate` summarised the rows (line 2)
/// example: One per cent at each end unless told.
/// > w.add(c1 = winsorize(wage)).aggregate(lowest = min(c1), highest = max(c1))
/// frame · 1 row × 2 variables
///
///       lowest   highest
///   1   9.1646   75.1261
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 3)
/// warning[W0201]: 26 rows with NA in c1 were skipped (line 3)
/// note: the panel declaration `worker × year` dropped: `aggregate` summarised the rows (line 3)
/// example: Within each group with `by:`: the cut points of each year.
/// > dataset(wages).add(capped = winsorize(wage, at: 0.05), by: year).aggregate(highest = max(capped), by: year).head(3)
/// frame · 3 rows × 2 variables
///
///       year   highest
///   1   2010     43.14
///   2   2011    47.352
///   3   2012   47.5785
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in wage were skipped (line 4)
/// warning[W0201]: 211 rows with NA in capped were skipped (line 4)
/// note: the panel declaration `worker × year` dropped: `aggregate` summarised the rows (line 4)
fn winsorize(x: Column<Float>, at: Float = 0.01) -> Column<Float> {
    if at < 0 || at > 0.5 {
        error(
            "`at:` of `winsorize` is a share between 0 and 0.5; above 0.5 the cut points would cross",
            code: Z0401,
            argument: "at",
        )
    }
    let f = frame(value = x)
    clamp(x, f.quantile(value, at), f.quantile(value, 1 - at))
}
